{
 "manufacturer": "Woodward",
 "manufacturer_slug": "woodward",
 "model": "easYgen-2000 Series genset controller (easYgen-2200 / 2300 / 2500)",
 "model_slug": "easygen-2000",
 "short_model": "easYgen-2000",
 "aliases": [
  "easYgen-2200",
  "easYgen-2300",
  "easYgen-2500",
  "easYgen-2000 Series"
 ],
 "equipment_type": "Generator controller",
 "applies_to": "easYgen-2200/2300/2500, data protocol 5100 (basic visualization), per manual 37535D.",
 "summary": "The easYgen-2000 series (2200/2300/2500) is Woodward's genset controller for single and paralleled sets. It is a Modbus RTU slave: data protocol 5100 is block-read from Modicon 450001, and parameters 503-510 take remote start/stop, acknowledge and interface setpoints. This map covers voltages, currents, frequencies, energy and hours, the operating state, control and alarm bit words, J1939 speed/pressure/hours and the remote-control parameters.",
 "transport": [
  "RTU"
 ],
 "defaults": {
  "unit_id": null,
  "baud": null,
  "parity": null,
  "data_bits": null,
  "stop_bits": null,
  "tcp_port": null
 },
 "addressing": {
  "convention": "Modicon",
  "note": "The 5100 table prints the Modicon address (450001) and a start address one lower (450000); the manual says 450001 may become bus address 50000, so the wire address is the Modicon number minus 400001. Parameters use Modicon 40000 + (parameter ID + 1): parameter 503 is 40504 = wire address 503. The manual says some PC tools need a shift of one (504)."
 },
 "default_word_order": "ABCD",
 "firmware": null,
 "source": {
  "title": "easYgen-2200/2300/2500 Genset Control - Manual",
  "doc_number": "37535D",
  "revision_date": "2016-08-02",
  "url": "https://www.woodward.com/products/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2024/08/37535_D.pdf",
  "pages": "pp. 407-411 (Modbus remote control and interface setpoints, high word first), Modbus addressing section of the interface chapter, pp. 471-484 (data protocol 5100)"
 },
 "notes": [
  "In the 5100 table, values occupying data bytes 3-6 are 32-bit (two registers); the others are 16-bit. The manual states 32-bit Modbus values go high word first (interface setpoint examples), so ABCD.",
  "The 5100 table does not say whether values are signed. Values that cannot be negative (voltages, currents, frequencies, energy, hours, speed, pressures, levels) are listed as unsigned, which reads them correctly either way. Left out for that reason: generator/mains real and reactive power, power factors, temperatures, engine torque, hours until maintenance and the active power / PF setpoint readbacks.",
  "Also left out: analog inputs/outputs (scaling 'changeable' or no sign), J1939 DM1/DM2 SPN entries and lamp words, and ECU-specific stop codes. The 5101 protocol (without J1939) is not covered.",
  "Remote start/stop needs the LogicsManager start request to include 04.13 Remote request. Interface setpoints only act when the AnalogManager source (05.06, 05.12, 05.03, 05.09) points at the interface value; no password is needed."
 ],
